	"<h3><strong>The Brass Fortresses, Volume 1</strong><br></h3>
	<strong><em>Author: Lack-Of-Grace</em></strong><br><br>
	
	 <br><br>The lands we attribute to the Rust, that is, these Rust Lands, as some intellectuals have called them, are nothing but a blemish on the beauty of Psydonia and, by extension, the world. I recall rather vividly the dae I had left the Brass Fortress Dytikos with nothing but my ceremonial rifle and vials of purification. I had enough drams to get me to the dwarven hold of Hammerhold and enough oil in my lamptern to get me through the viscous rust that defiles the land around Dytikos Pyrgos. <br><br>
	
	I think on the fourth dae, I had a dream where I was infected, maybe by some of the infected water that still lingered during purification. There was some root I had purified with my vial of Silver Curia so I cooked it together and had a meal of bouillon cubes with it. And some nice wine from home. By the Allfather, there are some times I miss home. The air is so fresh out here. I don’t need to worry about trading for supplements or worry about the insulation within… <br><br>
	
	After trekking outside of the Rust Lands that had surrounded Brass Fortress Dytikos, I came across a Rust Construct of some sort walking in a ceaseless, unending pattern during the hours of the nite. The Construct had worn the flesh of a great many creachures. During my studies of the construct, it noticed me and turned. I had heard stories from kinsmen in the lower levels that if you flashed a psycross at them, they’d have left you alone. It did. We conducted a mutual prayer to the Allfather… We departed rite after.<br><br>
	
	It had been but a few weeks now since I had departed Dytikos. I ran out of some bouillon cubes and had to settle for forging. There was a doe some odd distance from my camp so I decided to hunt it. It evaded me for a while but eventually I had fixed my bayonet and stabbed it in the throat. The meat tasted good, and I tested it for the plague rite after. Nothing. It was odd though, to taste meat that wasn’t repackaged flesh from a recycling bay. I never liked my rations, and often tymes I had to sneak a few herbs and spices ‘bout.. Just to feel something.<br><br>
	
	Roughly a dae before I was to arrive to Hammerhold, I came into contact with a mission of the Ten. They had assumed I was a warrior from another land not charted for they spoke highly of the bronze and steel I had worn as a protective measure against the Rust Beasts. Anyweys, they had noticed my psycross amulet which I had worn along my ring finger and assumed me to be a heretic. I professed my faith and fired off a warning shot to the Astratan as they readied an incantation. They seemed to have fled due to the sound of my rifle.<br><br>
	
	I should add I have never met an "Astratan," though travelers who had braved the Rustlands somehow spoke of the “Tennite” faith. The Lady had proclaimed there were no other Gods but the Allfather and that she and Ianoda were his left and right hands, so I thought he was a heretic myself. I should have killed him for his zealotry.<br><br>
	
	Eventually though, I had made it to Hammerhold. The ‘Astratan’ seems to have not told anyone or made me out as a bandit so customs had let me in once I revealed my status as a foreign dignitary. I was welcomed in and scholars from all walks of lyfe approached me asking me about the technology and crafts from the western spire. It was true, all of it. They were enamored. I kept the notions of our faith hidden, of course. That was bound to change in the coming yills though.<br><br>
	
	At the cusp of nite, I had retreated to my chambers and spoke a subtle prayer to the trinity of the Allfather. I was surrounded by all sorts of icons of faith dedicated to “Astrata” as they called her. I felt disgusted."
	]}